        "description": "<div class=\"no-overflow\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"></p>\r\n<h3 style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Exam Frequently Asked Questions</strong></h3>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<h5 style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Updated for A.Y. 2026/27</strong></h5>\r\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>GENERAL NOTE: </strong>The grades you obtain from the Competition and written exams <strong>are valid for the entire academic year</strong>. The exams are independent of each other, you can take them at different calls or retake only one of them. If you attend an exam session, the new grade you achieve for that specific exam (pass, fail, or withdrawn) <strong>will replace the previous one</strong>. Please note that while there is a minimum threshold to pass the Competition, there is no minimum grade for each individual written exam, and the exam is passed when the total score from the mandatory and optional parts is at least 18.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">When you register for an exam session, you will be asked which written exam you intend to take on the exam day or whether you only want to register a previous grade (typically from the Competition or a project). This information helps us prepare the appropriate number of exam papers for each type. This selection is not strict, therefore if you make a mistake you can still take a different exam. However, please note that if more than very few people change their exam selection on the day, we may not have enough exam papers available. Priority will be given to those who selected the exam correctly. Additionally, be cautious when choosing and completing your exam. For example, if you mistakenly take the Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam instead of the Full Theory exam, you will likely not be able to pass the course during that session.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">After each exam session you are registered for, <strong>I will publish a final course grade that reflects the *most recent* grades you have achieved in the written exams and Competition throughout the academic year</strong>. If your exam is incomplete (because you need or wish to take another written exam) or if you would like to attempt one of the exams again, <strong>you can simply reject the published grade, enroll in a subsequent exam session, and take (or retake) the written exam of your choice</strong> withoult losing any of the other grades.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<h3 style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>ENGAGED EXAM (COMPETITION):</strong></h3>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: center;\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I have passed the Competition and I want to register the grade as my final one without taking the optional Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Simply register for one of the exam sessions available during this academic year and, when prompted to select which exam you want to take, choose the option to register a previous grade (Competition). There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I have passed the Competition and I want to take the optional Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam later in the year. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Simply reject the grade I published and then take the optional Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam during any of the exam sessions of this academic year. There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I have passed the Competition but my grade is lower than 18<strong>. W</strong>hat should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: To pass the course, you need a grade of at least 18. You can achieve this by taking the optional Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam during any of the exam sessions this academic year and obtaining a grade high enough to reach 18. Alternatively, you can transition to the traditional exam format and take the Full Theory exam. If you have already passed the Competition, your rescaled grade will automatically be used in place of the optional Coding exam, unless you choose to take that written exam as well. The formula to convert the grade is in the slides deck where the course was presented.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I [<strong>obtained a low grade in</strong> / enrolled but did not attend / <strong>withdrew from</strong>] the Foundations &amp; Systems Theory exam. Can I take it again?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Yes, you can. Simply reject the grade published online and take the exam during any of the remaining exam sessions available this academic year. The Competition grade will remain valid for the entire academic year. There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I passed the Competition but I was not satisfied with the grade so I took the Full Theory exam but I [failed / withdrew / obtained a low grade], can I go back to the competition grade?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: No, once you participate in the Full Theory exam, you irreversibly transition to the traditional exam format. The Competition grade can only be used, if passed, as a replacement for the optional Coding exam. The formula to convert the grade is in the slides deck where the course was presented.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<h3 style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>TRADITIONAL EXAM:</strong></h3>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: center;\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I have passed the course by obtaining at least 18 in the Full Theory exam, but I want to take the optional Coding exam in one of the upcoming exam sessions. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Simply reject the grade published online and take the optional Coding exam during one of the available exam sessions this academic year, the grade of the Full Theory exam will remain valid for the whole academic year. There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I obtained a grade lower than 18 in the Full Theory exam. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: To pass the course, you need a grade of at least 18. You can either take the optional Coding exam during any of the exam sessions this academic year and achieve a grade high enough to reach 18, retake the Full Theory exam and obtain a passing grade, or a combination of both.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I am not satisfied with my grade and I wish to retake *only one* of the written exams without losing my grade in the other. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Simply reject the grade published online and take the desired exam during one of the available exam sessions this academic year. The grades of your other exams will remain valid for the entire academic year. There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: Why does the grade say I failed the exam? I only took the optional Coding exam and still need to take the Full Theory exam.</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: That is correct. Your current grade is not sufficient to pass the exam. However, since you enrolled in this exam session, I am required to publish the outcome.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I rejected a grade because I wanted to retry one of the exams but I have decided not to. What should I do?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: Simply register for one of the exam sessions available during this academic year, choose the option to register a previous grade. There is no need to inform me.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><strong>Q: I rejected a grade because I wanted to retry one of the exams, but then *after reading the exam paper* I decided to withdraw. Can I retain my old grade?</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">A: No. Once you attend a new exam session and accept the exam paper, you forfeit your previous grade, regardless of whether you complete the exam or withdraw.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h3 style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>REMOTE EXAM for illness, disability or international mobility:</strong></h3>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: center;\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">If you need to take a remote exam due to a <strong>serious illness, disability, or other significant personal condition</strong>, this is possible, but the request must be submitted through the Multichance service at Politecnico and be properly documented.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">If you are participating in an <strong>international mobility exchange</strong>, you have the option to take the written exam at your host institution instead of at Politecnico di Milano. <span style=\"font-size: 0.875rem;\">To do so, you are required <strong>to find a local professor at your host institution who can supervise the exam</strong>. The international student office of the local university may be able to help you. Please connect me with this professor so that I can provide them with the exam materials. After the exam, the supervising professor should send me a digital copy of your completed exam. While I understand that finding a local professor may not be easy, this requirement ensures consistency and fairness for all students.</span></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Ideally, the exam should be scheduled simultaneously with the exam at Politecnico. However, if time zone differences make this impractical, the exam can be taken at a different time or on a date close to the official exam date.</p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Please note that <strong>your international mobility status will be verified</strong>, and <strong>being abroad for personal reasons does not qualify for taking the exam remotely</strong>. </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p></div>",
